## 6. Repository structure (used consistently across all phase docs)

```
financial-intel-platform/
├── apps/
│   ├── api/                    # FastAPI gateway + orchestrator entrypoint
│   └── web/                    # Next.js frontend (Phase 3)
├── packages/
│   ├── contracts/              # Shared, versioned Pydantic schemas
│   ├── agents/
│   │   ├── market_data/
│   │   ├── financials/
│   │   ├── news/
│   │   ├── sentiment/
│   │   ├── sector/
│   │   ├── risk/
│   │   ├── forecast/
│   │   └── report/
│   ├── orchestrator/           # LangGraph graph definitions, nodes, edges
│   └── infra/                  # DB models/migrations, cache client, external API clients, resilience utils
├── docs/
│   ├── adr/                    # One markdown file per decision
│   ├── architecture.md
│   ├── runbook.md
│   ├── incident-response.md
│   ├── testing-strategy.md
│   ├── monitoring-guide.md
│   └── onboarding.md
├── tests/
│   ├── unit/
│   ├── contract/
│   ├── integration/
│   ├── e2e/
│   ├── load/
│   └── chaos/
├── docker/
│   ├── docker-compose.yml
│   └── Dockerfile.api
├── .github/workflows/
│   └── ci.yml
└── pyproject.toml
```

This structure stays the same from Phase 1 through Phase 4. Phase 5 is the only phase that reorganizes it (into per-service repos or a monorepo with per-service deploy configs) — and only if the evidence from Phase 2's metrics justifies it.

---

## 7. Deliverable standard

By the end, the repo should read like an internal platform built by a quant research / investment intelligence engineering team, and should be defensible in an interview as: "here's the problem, here are the alternatives I considered, here's why I chose this, here's what it costs me, here's how I'd operate it at 3am when it breaks."

Next: open `01_phase1_monolith_foundation.md` and start there.
